 # SDF-based Neural Fields
 Also called: SDF NeRF, Signed Distance Function Neural Fields
+
 ## Overview
-SDF-based Neural Fields replace the volumetric density prediction of standard NeRFs with a Signed Distance Function (SDF) to provide a more explicit and smooth formulation of surfaces [S1, unofficial]. This approach addresses the geometric inaccuracy and surface holes typical of density-based NeRFs by defining geometry via a zero-level set rather than an integrated opacity field [S1, unofficial].
+SDF-based Neural Fields replace the implicit density field of standard NeRFs with a Signed Distance Function (SDF) to provide a more explicit and smooth formulation of surfaces [S1, unofficial]. This approach addresses the geometric inaccuracy and surface holes typical of density-based NeRFs by leveraging the zero-level set of the SDF to define geometry [S1, unofficial].
+
 ## Shared principles
 The core mechanism involves predicting a continuous scalar field $s: \mathbb{R}^3 \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ instead of a density $\sigma(\mathbf{x})$ [S1, unofficial]. For any 3D point $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^3$, the function value $s(\mathbf{x})$ represents the distance to the closest surface, with the sign conventionally negative inside the object and positive outside [S1, unofficial]. The surface is defined as the zero-level set $\{\mathbf{x} \mid s(\mathbf{x}) = 0\}$ [S1, unofficial].
 
 To enable volumetric rendering, the predicted SDF value $s(\mathbf{x})$ is converted into a density $\sigma(\mathbf{x})$ [S1, unofficial]. The specific conversion function is not detailed in the provided sources [G]. Standard differentiable rendering pipelines then integrate this density along rays to produce images, while the SDF structure encourages sharper, more coherent surface reconstructions compared to soft volumetric boundaries [S1, unofficial].
+
